你还记得第一次看试播集时的反应吗？克雷格，同样的问题，看完之后呢？是的，我不记得我第一次看了，我不记得我是不是收到过链接，我想帕姆可能邀请过我们作为演员去观看，你还记得吗，克雷格，你记得放映吗？我本想说，我不知道你有没有收到链接，你可能收到了一盘该死的录像带，哦，是的，我不知道，我不记得了，但我记得，是的，然后我最终和观众一起看了好多次，因为我们知道，为了收听笑声，我们和观众一起看了。

00:19:50.559 --> 00:19:51.799
I think, you know, I saw it a bunch.
我想，你知道的，我看了很多次。

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:55.960
I've seen the pilot probably more than any other episode.
我看的试播集可能比其他任何一集都多。

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:56.799
Yeah.
是的。

00:19:56.799 --> 00:19:59.160
And Greg, you obviously saw it all through the editing process.
格雷格，你显然在整个剪辑过程中都看过了。

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.000
It's different for me because we edited the hell out of it.
对我的感觉不一样，因为我们对它进行了地狱般的剪辑。

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:02.960
You know, it wasn't like eight minutes up.
你知道的，它可不是说八分钟就完事了。

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.759
You know, it was like, it was supposed to be 22 minutes.
你知道的，它本应该是22分钟。

00:20:04.759 --> 00:20:06.319
It was 31 or whatever it was.
结果是31分钟还是别的什么。

00:20:06.319 --> 00:20:09.240
And we had to, we spent weeks whittling it and editing down.
我们不得不花了好几个星期去精简和剪辑。

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:16.200
At the weirdest part was watching a focus group test audience watch it with their dials of like, like, and don't like.
最诡异的部分是看着焦点小组测试观众观看它，他们手里拿着那种表示喜欢和不喜欢的表决旋钮。

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:25.920
And these dials that are all averaged together of this room for the people watching it in real time, you see the lines going up and down of male approval or disapproval and female approval or disapproval.
这些旋钮的数据在这个房间里实时平均显示出来，你可以看到代表男性认可或不认可、女性认可或不认可的线条上下波动。

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:31.519
I think one's blue and one's pink in a stereotypical fashion.
我想一个是蓝色，一个是粉色，挺有刻板印象风格的。

00:20:31.519 --> 00:20:34.640
And it's like, ooh, women like that moment, men did not.
就像是，哦，女性喜欢那个时刻，男性不喜欢。

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:37.519
And people, it's just so weird.
而且大家，这真的太奇怪了。

00:20:37.519 --> 00:20:43.119
So I watched the pilot every way you could possibly watch the pilot and it was super weird, some of them.
所以我通过所有可能的方式看了一遍试播集，而且有些方式超级奇怪。

00:20:43.119 --> 00:20:51.200
But then when we had it locked or almost locked and showed it to a live audience and recorded their laughs and their responses, which was what aired in the show.
但后来当我们把剪辑敲定或接近敲定，并把它放给现场观众看、记录他们的笑声和反应时——这就是节目中播出的内容。

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:55.880
For that one, that was fantastic because people, you knew it worked.
对于那一次，这太棒了，因为你知道，观众的反应说明它成功了。

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:56.720
You knew it worked.
你知道它成功了。

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:03.000
I knew it worked before that because everybody that came into the edit room and watched it, every moment that had to work worked.
在那之前我就知道它成功了，因为所有走进剪辑室观看的人，每一个必须起效的时刻都起效了。

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:13.079
So I felt confident, but the best moment was finally getting to show a two-in- audience full of humans and not assessing it in any way they were watching it and reacting naturally.
所以我感到很有信心，但最好的时刻终于还是向满满一屋子真实人类的观众展示，他们没有任何先入为主地观看，并做出自然的反应。

00:21:13.079 --> 00:21:21.240
How much Craig at the time, and this might have changed over the course, the show became more successful and you more confident in this new crazy job that you had.
克雷格当时受到的影响有多大——随着节目变得更成功，你对这份疯狂的新工作更有自信，这种情况可能会有所改变。

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:29.640
How much of these other voices, whether it's audience reaction or test audience reaction or executives, how much is actually influencing your writing of the show?
这些其他声音，无论是观众的反应、试播观众的反应还是高管的意见，实际上在多大程度上影响了你对节目的编写？

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:32.890
Yeah, I mean, I think a good note is a good note.
是的，我的意思是，我认为好的意见就是好意见。

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:39.380
I think we had some very smart executives from both the studio and the network who were really believed in the show and supported it.
我认为我们有一些来自制片厂和电视网的非常聪明的行政人员，他们真的很相信这个节目并支持它。

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:39.880
it.
它。

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:49.022
And it took some time, sometimes for people for them to get on board, but sort of have serialized the show was and sort of emotional and stuff like that.
而且这需要一些时间，有时需要人们去适应，比如这个节目是几集连贯的序列剧，还有那种情感等等。

00:21:49.022 --> 00:21:52.608
But for the most part, they just want to spend for the show.
但大多数情况下，他们只是想为节目出力。

00:21:52.608 --> 00:21:58.285
And it was actually very helpful because we didn't shoot it in front of a live studio audience.
这其实非常有用，因为我们并不是在现场观众面前拍摄的。

00:21:58.285 --> 00:22:00.376
So those responses meant something.
所以那些反应是有意义的。

00:22:00.376 --> 00:22:11.730
If we got notes about confusion or boredom or something was slow and just it came from that human like, And executive is saying to me, I felt confused here, or this felt really rushed to me.
如果我们收到关于困惑、无聊或者觉得节奏慢的意见，那正是来自于那种人类的直觉，比如高管对我说：“我在这里感到困惑”，或者“我觉得这部分节奏太急促了”。

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:12.410
That's good.
这很好。

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:13.410
That's really useful.
这真的很有用。

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:14.410
That's a human reaction.
这是一种人类的反应。

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:16.809
They felt that in their gut and they told us.
他们内心感受到了这一点，并告诉了我们。

00:22:16.809 --> 00:22:26.769
The other thing that was really useful, and I think Carter pointed this out in his episode, the crew reacted when we'd perform these scenes, the actors would perform these scenes.
另一件非常有用的事，我想卡特在他的那一集里也提到过，就是当演员们表演这些场景时，剧组工作人员的反应。

00:22:26.769 --> 00:22:33.490
And the crew wouldn't have read every line of every script or like the new rewrite or whatever that came out the night before we shot.
剧组并没有读过每一份剧本的每一行台词，也没有读过我们在拍摄前一晚写出的新重写台词之类的东西。

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:35.289
And they'd react honestly to stuff.
他们会对内容做出真实的反应。

00:22:35.289 --> 00:22:36.289
They'd really laugh.
他们真的会笑。

00:22:36.289 --> 00:22:37.289
That's the only way.
这是唯一的途径。

00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:38.289
And go cricket silence.
或者一片死寂。

00:22:38.289 --> 00:22:40.930
And then it was only that first time.
而且那只代表第一次的反应。

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:43.450
So you had to aggregate responses.
所以你必须综合各种反应。

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:46.049
You're getting some responses from different places.
你从不同的地方获得一些反馈。

00:22:46.049 --> 00:22:47.049
And you aggregate them.
然后你把它们综合起来。

00:22:47.049 --> 00:22:52.009
And especially if you see link ups, and you're like, oh, that didn't work in three different settings now.
特别是如果你发现某些问题有关联，就会想，哦，这在三个不同的场合下都没起作用。

00:22:52.009 --> 00:22:55.369
It didn't work at the table read, the crew didn't laugh, et cetera, et cetera.
它在围读时没起作用，剧组人员没笑，诸如此类。

00:22:55.369 --> 00:22:58.890
And you sort of had to take the hit and go, okay, let's take this as a sign.
你基本上得接受这个打击，然后想，好吧，把这当作一个信号。

00:22:58.890 --> 00:22:59.890
We may need to change it.
我们可能需要改变它。

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:08.849
Some stuff you got to believe in, even if it doesn't play on its feet in front of the crew, you say, this is a quiet moment we can, well, this will work in the other room.
有些东西你必须坚信，即使它在剧组面前现场演出来没有效果，你也会说，这是一个安静的时刻，我们可以，嗯，这在另一个场景里会起作用。

00:23:08.849 --> 00:23:10.089
There's a different kind of moment.
那是另一种时刻。

00:23:10.089 --> 00:23:12.569
So it's a case by case.
所以这得具体情况具体分析。

00:23:12.569 --> 00:23:23.410
And Josh, I assume you're basically at this point insulated from the larger, louder voices about the show should be more of this, the stuff that Craig and Carter are hearing.
乔伊，我想在这一点上，你基本上已经隔绝了那些关于“节目应该更偏向哪种风格”的更大、更吵闹的声音，也就是克雷格和卡特所听到的那些。

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:30.769
you in your process early on, can you talk a little bit about what is affecting you?
在你的早期创作过程中，你能聊聊是什么在影响你吗？

00:23:30.769 --> 00:23:46.289
Because unlike a play where you're working on this one character and you know it's got to be contained within this two hours, you do have it in somewhere in your head of like the choices that I'm making for this character could live for years, could live as it turns out for a decade.
因为不像戏剧，你只需要琢磨这一个角色，并且知道它必须包含在这两个小时内，但在你的脑海里某处，你会意识到你为这个角色做出的选择可能会持续多年，事实证明甚至可以持续十年。

00:23:46.289 --> 00:23:50.450
What's happening as you're developing that early on, obviously with the scripts that you're being handed?
在你早期发展这个角色的过程中，显然要结合你拿到的剧本，究竟发生了什么？

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:58.960
Yeah, I mean, I think any sort of notes from the Studio Network, I was super insulated from that on the pilot at the very least.
是的，我的意思是，我认为来自制片厂和电视网的任何意见，至少在试播集时，我都完全被隔绝在外了。

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:07.240
Then you end up getting to know the Studio Network executives, and they're there every week, and you become really friendly with them.
然后你最终会开始了解制片厂和电视网的高管，他们每周都在那儿，你和他们变得非常熟悉。

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:11.400
They never felt like the enemy was walking on to stage, you know.
你绝不会觉得敌人走进了片场，你知道的。

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:16.799
But I was very interested in process and in writing.
但我对创作过程和编剧工作非常感兴趣。

00:24:16.799 --> 00:24:24.359
So I remember, I was probably alone, well, among the cast members, and Craig can tell me if I'm wrong, I was probably the most curious about process.
所以我记得，在演员当中我可能算是个例外——克雷格可以告诉我我有没有记错——我对创作过程大概是最好奇的。

00:24:24.359 --> 00:24:29.720
I was probably the one who asked the most about what was coming up and what you guys were working on and what you were struggling with.
我可能也是那个问得最多的人，比如接下来要发生什么，你们在创作什么，你们遇到了什么困难。

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:33.799
And like, I was just, I had an endless appetite for those kinds of questions.
而且我就是，我对这类问题有着无尽的渴望。

00:24:33.799 --> 00:24:36.400
Some of it was like a little bit like, what's coming down the pike?
其中有些感觉就像是，接下来会有什么发展？

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:38.440
Like I wanted to, you know, you become a fan of the show too.
就像我想，你知道的，你也成了这个节目的粉丝。

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:43.799
I was like, I want to know what happens to this guy that I'm playing.
我就想，我想知道我演的这个家伙最后怎么样了。

00:24:43.799 --> 00:24:50.839
But you know, I still was a relative newcomer to the world of filmed entertainment.
但你知道，我对电影电视这个世界来说，相对还是个新人。

00:24:50.839 --> 00:24:58.680
I had done it so much theater since I was 15 and theater, the mercy of theater and somewhat of the sadness of theater is it's a femoral.
我从15岁起演了那么多话剧，而话剧的残酷之处——某种程度上也是话剧的悲哀——在于它是短暂的。

00:24:58.680 --> 00:24:59.680
It goes away.
它转瞬即逝。

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:01.400
You do it and then you have to keep doing it.
你演完了，然后你得继续演下一场。

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:05.039
It's much more like a restaurant where you have to keep making the food every night, you know?
它更像是一家餐馆，你每天晚上都得不停地做饭，你知道吗？

00:25:05.039 --> 00:25:06.039
Yeah.
是的。

00:25:06.039 --> 00:25:11.879
And you don't have to be haunted by the bad night's performance or even get to celebrate that much the good night's for.
你不用为表现糟糕的夜晚而耿耿于怀，甚至也没时间为表现出色的夜晚庆祝太久。

00:25:11.879 --> 00:25:14.799
It's just it, it evanesc is that the word it just goes away.
它就是，它会消逝，是这个词吗？它就是消失了。

00:25:14.799 --> 00:25:15.299
Right.
对吧。

00:25:15.299 --> 00:25:28.239
And with filmed entertainment, uh, I, I found it took me years to, um, become less self conscious and to not think about the kind of like museum quality of it that it would, it would last forever.
而在影视表演方面，呃，我发现我花了多年时间，嗯，才变得不那么自我意识过剩，不再去想那种博物馆品质般的东西——它是会永远保留下去的。

00:25:28.239 --> 00:25:36.095
And I always, always, always wanted another take if one was available because I just wanted another whack at the thing.
而且我总是、总是、总是想要再拍一条，如果有机会的话，因为我只是想再试一次。

00:25:36.095 --> 00:25:39.395
But, um, it wasn't until I directed my own movies.
但是，嗯，直到我自己导演了电影。

00:25:39.395 --> 00:25:43.819
And I was in both the movies I directed where I had to edit myself.
而且我拍的两部电影我都参演了，我还得自己剪辑。

00:25:43.819 --> 00:25:45.271
And I learned so much.
我学到了太多。

00:25:45.271 --> 00:25:45.931
I learned.
我学到了。

00:25:45.931 --> 00:25:46.789
It was weird.
这感觉很奇妙。

00:25:46.789 --> 00:25:51.806
It's something happened to me where I was like, I know what I look like now.
我身上发生了一些变化，当时我就想：我现在知道自己长什么样了。

00:25:51.806 --> 00:26:02.237
You know when you're a kid and you hear your voice on tape for the first time and you're like, horrified, you're like, that can't be what I sound like, right?
你知道当你还是个孩子的时候，第一次在磁带里听到自己的声音，你会觉得毛骨悚然，你会想：我的声音听起来绝不可能是这样的，对吧？

00:26:02.237 --> 00:26:11.743
You go through that a little bit when you see yourself on camera, like Craig and Carter as the fireman or the EMT guys, but you still haunts me.
当你第一次在镜头里看到自己时，也会经历这种感觉，就像Craig和Carter演的消防员或者急救人员那样，但这仍然让我挥之不去。

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:17.940
There was this feeling once I edited myself, I was like, I didn't think I looked particularly good or bad.
自我剪辑过一次后，我有一种感觉，我觉得自己看起来并没有特别好或特别坏。

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:21.839
I just knew what I looked like and I had a kind of ruthless acceptance of it.
我只是知道了自己长什么样，并且对它有一种无情的接受。

00:26:21.839 --> 00:26:25.079
And since I've done that, I have a much easier time watching myself on screen.
自从我做了这件事之后，我在银幕上看着自己时就轻松多了。

00:26:25.079 --> 00:26:30.559
It still pops up where you're like, I don't know or I wish they would have chosen a different take or something.
有时候还是会冒出一些想法，比如：我不知道啊，或者我希望他们当时选的是另一条素材之类的。

00:26:30.559 --> 00:26:32.160
And it's hard.
这真的很难。

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:33.160
It's hard.
太难了。

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:40.119
I think that people don't understand how much self-consciousness is induced by acting on screen.
我觉得人们并不理解，在镜头前表演会带来多么严重的自我意识过剩。

00:26:40.119 --> 00:26:48.400
And when I see people, you know, like Lizzy Olson, who was in my first, in my second movie, Liberal Arts, like, she was just, she's just two of the men are born.
当我看到那些人，你知道的，比如Lizzy Olson，她演了我的第一部、我的第二部电影《艺术学院》，她简直天生就是吃这碗饭的。

00:26:48.400 --> 00:27:03.960
I think Kobe was like this too, like they're just comfortable being photographed and, you know, fussed over in a way that I, coming from the theater and I don't know, just my general kind of level of self-consciousness back in those days, I had a harder time relaxing.
我觉得Kobe也是这样，他们就是能泰然自若地被拍照，然后被人众星捧月般地伺候着。而我，出身于戏剧界，而且不知道为什么，加上我当年那种普遍的自我意识过剩，我很难彻底放松下来。

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:09.640
I feel like I know how to act in front of the camera in a totally different way than I did 20 years ago.
我觉得我现在在镜头前表演的方式，和20年前完全不同了，我知道该怎么演了。

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:10.640
All right.
好吧。

